      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="notes">
        <tei:p>Israel Zangwill’s translation of Omnam Kén by Rabbi Yom Tov of York (Joigny), was first published albeit incomplete, as “Atonement Hymn” in <tei:hi rend="italic">Blind Children: Poems</tei:hi> (1903), pp. 134-135. Introducing the piyyut, Zangwill notes, “The translation of this curiosity of literature preserves, without adding or subtracting a single word, the precise metre, rhyme-scheme, and alphabetical acrostic of the twelfth-century Hebrew original.” A year later, it was published complete, opposite Hebrew verse, in Arthur Davis and Herbert Adler’s <tei:hi rend="italic">Maḥzor Avodat Ohel Moed: Avodat Yom haKippurim (Evening Service)</tei:hi> (1904), p. 38.</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
